# General Development Rules (.mdc)

## 1. Visual Design Consistency (Even Grid & Button Rule)
- **Even Metric/KPI Cards Grid:** All KPI / Metric Summary Cards rendered side-by-side MUST use equal weights (`Modifier.weight(1f)`) to form a perfectly balanced, even grid.
- **Even Button Combinations:** Action buttons rendered side-by-side MUST use equal weights (`Modifier.weight(1f)`).
- **Consistent Design Tokens:** Use standardized card shapes (`RoundedCornerShape(16.dp)`), button shapes (`RoundedCornerShape(12.dp)`), and brand colors (`PrimaryGreen` `#1B5E20`, `WarmGold` `#C9A227`, `SurfaceWhite` `#FFFFFF`).

## 2. View-First UX Pattern (View Mode $\rightarrow$ Edit Mode)
- **View-First Pattern:** Always render settings, profiles, and organization info in a clean, formatted View Mode first.
- **Explicit Edit Action:** Include an explicit "Edit Profile / Settings" button (✏️) to switch to Edit Mode. Never leave raw, permanently editable text input fields exposed by default on view screens.
- **Save & Cancel Handlers:** Edit Mode must provide clear "Save Changes" and "Cancel" buttons. Saving must update the state repository dynamically and return to View Mode.

## 3. DRY Principle & Code Reusability
- **Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY):** NEVER duplicate business logic, validation rules, or UI components. Reuse existing components in `presentation/common/` and domain models.
- **Single Source of Truth:** Keep a single, centralized source of truth for string dictionaries (`Strings.kt`), currency/digit formatters (`CurrencyFormatter`), and theme design tokens (`Theme.kt`).

## 4. Language & Localization
- Write idiomatic Kotlin code with strict null safety and coroutines.
- All user-facing UI texts, digits (`০, ১, ২...`), and currency (`৳`) MUST support dynamic Bengali and English localization. No hardcoded English strings in UI components.

## 5. Tool & File Safety
- **NEVER** use destructive shell commands (`rm`, `sed`). Always use built-in IDE editing tools (`write_file`, `replace_file_content`).
- Make small, targeted edits. Do not pollute or modify unrelated files.
